          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "py1kucr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Crust"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kyvo7s2",
      "content": "• The crust is the outermost layer of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sjwwpm0",
      "content": "• It is very thin compared to other layers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p9c4l7h",
      "content": "• Average thickness is about 35 km under continents."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v78al4m",
      "content": "• Oceanic crust is thinner (5–10 km)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vxn56c6",
      "content": "• Main elements are oxygen, silicon, aluminum, iron and calcium."
    },
    {
      "id": "ibxke0g",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Crust"
    },
    {
      "id": "l5hco85",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Thickness</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Composition</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Continental Crust</p>",
            "<p>30–70 km</p>",
            "<p>Granite rocks rich in silica and alumina</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Oceanic Crust</p>",
            "<p>5–10 km</p>",
            "<p>Basalt rocks rich in silica and magnesium</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "04v14fk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uqoex7b",
      "content": "• The mantle lies below the crust and extends up to about 2900 km depth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3w2zpqf",
      "content": "• It forms nearly 84% of Earth's volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Layer",
        "Dominant Elements",
        "Physical State"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Crust",
          "Silicon & Aluminium (Sial) / Silicon & Magnesium (Sima)",
          "Solid"
        ],
        [
          "Mantle",
          "Silicon, Iron, Magnesium (Sima/Silicates)",
          "Solid / Semi-fluid (Asthenosphere)"
        ],
        [
          "Outer Core",
          "Nickel & Iron (Nife)",
          "Liquid"
        ],
        [
          "Inner Core",
          "Nickel & Iron (Nife)",
          "Solid"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "qb28cwk"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zhby5ru",
      "content": "• The upper part of the mantle with the crust forms the lithosphere."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g69p8i0",
      "content": "• Below it lies the asthenosphere which contains partially molten rocks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mh12leq",
      "content": "• The mantle plays a major role in plate tectonic movements."
    },
    {
      "id": "xfat3kn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Zones in the Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8d4i7xg",
      "content": "• Lithosphere – rigid outer layer including crust and upper mantle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "foregzx",
      "content": "• Asthenosphere – semi-molten layer where tectonic plates float."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o3xvoz6",
      "content": "• Mesosphere – lower mantle with higher density."
    },
    {
      "id": "kmtfv5d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fpr7ncp",
      "content": "• The core is the innermost part of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "owu0dxc",
      "content": "• It extends from 2900 km to the center at 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "eddxf8v",
      "content": "• It is mainly composed of iron and nickel."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "11iot05",
      "content": "• The core is responsible for generating Earth's magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "id": "6xfq2kk",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Memorize the order of discontinuities from surface to center: Conrad, Mohorovičić (Moho), Repetti, Gutenberg, and Lehmann.",
        "Distinguish between Continental Crust (Sial - Silicon & Aluminium) and Oceanic Crust (Sima - Silicon & Magnesium).",
        "Outer core is liquid while Inner core is solid due to extreme pressure despite high temperatures."
      ],
      "id": "cot9tg0"
    },
